Description:
Part 3, plate 3 from a series of pattern or copybook sheets: an arrangement mammals, large and small, including a large monkey with fruit at left centre, a rhinoceros near the centre, a goat at top centre, a lion and two rabbits at upper right (one rabbit not visible due to paper loss), a boar and three more monkeys to the right of the centre, dogs and a buck (stag) at lower left, and elephant with its leg around a tree trunk at lower right.
Engraving
Portions of the sheet missing at upper and lower righthand corners, affecting the image.;

A satire on pretensions to elegance showing a fine room in which a thin elderly dancing master teaches  a stout young woman to dance, while an older woman sitting behind  watches them.
A man sits playing the lute to the left, books and sheets of music lie on the floor; a monkey plays with a fan; a little dog capers on its hind legs behind the dancing pair. On the wall behind are two pictures in ornate frames, one of a dancing couple and the other of a bear dancing with its keeper.  1 January 1768
Etching and engraving;

An illustration to Johnson's 'The Rambler' for Harrison's 'British Classicks'; after Stothard; oval composition with basket of flowers at the top and plaque for the book title below.
Plate I; A winged man with a sword coming to the rescue of a man and his  dog who are attacked by a crocodile in the right foreground, while a  shepherd on the bank hides his face in fear.  1785
Etching and engraving;

